// Solution — Module 01, Exercise 02: Overflow and Wrapping
//
// Task 1: the warning is
//   spooked at line N: u8 overflow: 255 + 1 = 256, wrapped to 0 (range [0, 255])
//
// Task 2: u8(255) + u8(1) is 0. The arithmetic is done, the result does not
// fit in eight bits, and the high bit falls off the end. In C this is defined
// behaviour for unsigned types — which is exactly why it is dangerous: the
// compiler is allowed to assume you meant it.

counter = u8(254)
print(f"start: {counter}")

counter = counter + u8(1)
print(f"after +1: {counter}")

counter = counter + u8(1)
print(f"after +1 again: {counter}")

// Task 3: count from 250 up through 260.
//
// The loop runs ten times, but the warning fires exactly ONCE — on the single
// step that crosses 255. Every other step is an ordinary addition. This is the
// point of the exercise: a wrap is a one-instant event, easy to miss in a log,
// and afterwards the counter looks perfectly reasonable again.
print("")
print("counting u8 from 250, ten times:")

c = u8(250)
i = 0
while i < 10 {
    c = c + u8(1)
    print(f"  step {i + 1}: {c}")
    i = i + 1
}
print(f"final value: {c}")

// 250 + 10 = 260, and 260 - 256 = 4. The counter ends at 4, not 260.

// BONUS: signed overflow.
//
// In C, signed overflow is *undefined* behaviour, not merely wrapping — the
// compiler may assume it never happens and optimise on that basis. crunch-c
// wraps and warns so you can see the shape of the bug.
print("")
neg = i8(-128)
print(f"i8 min: {neg}")

neg = neg - i8(1)
print(f"after -1: {neg}")

// -128 - 1 wraps to 127: the lowest value becomes the highest.
